module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_rust_test_target_that_does_not_compile_reads_green

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, decl_ref }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundBelowTheLadder, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { MechanicallyPreventable }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }

// A RUST TEST MODULE CAN SIT IN THE SEED UNBUILDABLE AND NO REQUIRED CHECK CAN SAY SO. Found 2026-10-29
// by the manager's own directive, while a v1 seed repair (the iterative `Value` release, PR #15360) was
// being measured: the PR's Rust evidence is tests, and the first thing that ever compiled those tests
// was a manual `cargo test --no-run` on the shared runner. Filed here at the manager's direction
// (tidy-ant-251, 2026-10-29) as its own row, kept out of that PR because the invalid state is about the
// required path and that PR is about the interpreter. CI scope belongs to Manager 2, and the gate step
// is crisp-bee-97's to build; this row files the class and names the trigger, and builds nothing.

data a_rust_test_target_that_does_not_compile_reads_green: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_rust_test_target_that_does_not_compile_reads_green" as NonEmptyStr,

  // FOUND SILENTLY WRONG, SO NOT ON THE LADDER AT ALL. The state reports nothing: no refusal, no failing
  // test, no diagnostic on any required surface. A failing test at least speaks; an unbuildable test
  // target does not exist as far as the required path is concerned, and silent wrongness is below the
  // ladder rather than a rung on it. The ceiling is MechanicallyPreventable, not aspirational: one
  // required step that compiles the test target refuses the state at the earliest boundary that cannot
  // be justified, which is where §6b puts the fix.
  rung: RungRead {
    found_at: FoundBelowTheLadder,
    standing: BelowCeiling {
      current: FoundBelowTheLadder,
      ceiling: MechanicallyPreventable,
      blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt,
      trigger_capability: "the capability of the required path to compile the seed's Rust test targets, restored by the v2-bootstrap step that runs cargo test --no-run, modeled in gunbc.witness.compiler_gate_workflow and counted once it is measured on the fleet, SUFFICIENT FOR a checkout whose #[cfg(test)] code fails to name-resolve to refuse the merge while remaining INDIFFERENT to whether any test passes -- which is exactly why it narrows `gunbc.rung_drop.v1_required_lanes_withdrawn` rather than retiring it"
    },
  },

  receipts: [
    "**INVALID STATE.** A module under `#[cfg(test)]` in the seed Rust does not compile — its `use` list
    omits names its own bodies call — and every required check on the pull request is consistent with
    that. Not a red test: a red test is evidence. This is test source that cannot be built, committed as
    source, and invisible. The harm is precise: a PR whose stated evidence is 'the tests prove it' can
    carry tests that were never built by anything the repo requires, so the word 'proven' in a PR body
    can mean 'written'. The class covers the seed's `src/v1/stage0/src/*.rs` test modules and every
    `[[test]]`/integration target the workspace declares.",

    "**WHY IT LOOKS SAFE AND ISN'T.** `cargo build` type-checks the library with `#[cfg(test)]` code
    stripped before name resolution, so unresolved names, wrong arities and nonexistent types inside a
    test module are never read. The required path is only that compile: the commands the workflows run
    are enumerated by a grep anyone can re-derive, `grep -rn \"cargo test\" .github/workflows/` returning
    nothing while the same files contain `cargo build --release -p v1-compiler --bin gunbc` and its
    `--bin claim_executor` variant. The pre-push hook (`.githooks/pre-push`) is `cargo fmt --all --check`
    plus a binary and `instrument_targets` discovery check, neither of which builds tests. So there is
    no path from 'the PR is green' to 'the test target compiles', and every green surface — fmt, the
    hook, `v2-bootstrap`, heal, the seed census — is fully consistent with the tests being dead source.",

    "**HOW IT WAS FOUND, which is the incident that earns the row: by accident, on the way to measuring
    something else.** A Rust RED/GREEN re-run for the `Value`-drop repair died at its build step with the
    `v1-compiler (lib test)` target refusing to compile, while the same source's library compile and the
    PR's required checks had passed at the previous head. The names all existed in the file: `HamtMap` is
    a root-module alias and `map_value` and `CanonKey` are root-module items. What differed was the
    importing module: the test module that used them opens an explicit `use super::{...}` list, while the
    sibling test that uses the same names successfully lives in a *different* module
    (`portable_canonical_order_tests`) which opens `use super::*`. Copying a working form across a module
    boundary is exactly the edit a lib-only compile cannot see, and it survived one review round and one
    approval before a manual `cargo test --no-run` on the runner read it.",

    "**THE MEASURED SPECIMEN, and the pair that must be re-derived rather than quoted.** Take the commit
    that carries the broken module (`a853c8e91532` on `repair/value-drop-weak-held-carriers`) on a clean
    checkout and run, in this order, the two commands whose difference IS the class:
    A `cargo build --release --locked -p v1-compiler --bin gunbc --bin claim_executor` — verbatim the
    required-path command from `.github/workflows`, and the one that must come back clean for the row's
    premise to hold; and
    B `cargo test --no-run --locked -p v1-compiler --features trmc_runtime` — which no required job runs,
    and which refuses the same source with `error[E0425]: cannot find function map_value in this scope`,
    three `error[E0433]: failed to resolve: use of undeclared type` on `HamtMap`/`CanonKey`, and
    `could not compile v1-compiler (lib test) due to 4 previous errors`. A=0 with B refusing is the
    invalid state itself, measured on one tree. The commands are the instrument; the scratch log of my
    own run is not, and no number from it is being asked of any reader. Where the pair has not been run,
    the row's claim is the command set, which is checkable in `.github/workflows`.",

    "**CONSUMPTION.** The derived `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.roster` `recurring_failure_mode_roster`
    includes this per-class declaration, and the consumer that reads the rung is the instrument
    `gunbc test //gunbc/instruments:rfm-rung-census` (`gunbc.instruments.rfm_rung_census`), whose own header
    states what it refuses: a `BelowCeiling` whose `current` is at or above its `ceiling`, and a trigger
    whose capability is blank or spelled as a lifecycle-bounded referent. So the rung reading above is
    answered by that census and not by prose here. This row adds no repair, builds no gate step, and
    registers nothing by hand: the roster is derived, and the generated-artifact phase is the wall that
    refuses when committed bytes and generated bytes disagree.",

    "**CEILING AND TRIGGER, per the ruling on this row (clever-crane-830 via sleek-owl-397).** The
    next-rung trigger is a STEP in `v2-bootstrap`, modeled in `gunbc.witness.compiler_gate_workflow`,
    that runs `cargo test --no-run`; it counts as a step on the ladder only once it is measured on the
    fleet, and `crisp-bee-97` owns building it. The blocker class is ClimbableButUnbuilt rather than
    AwaitsOneGrounding because nothing here waits on an authority or a grounding: the mechanism is a
    toolchain command that exists today, and what it lacks is a lane that puts it on the required path.
    **This trigger is declared a NARROWING of `gunbc.rung_drop.v1_required_lanes_withdrawn`, not a
    retirement of it.** That drop stays standing for what it was dropped for: `--no-run` proves the tests
    *compile* and proves nothing about whether they *pass*, so the withdrawn lane's verdict semantics are
    not restored by this step and must not be read as restored. The step narrows the drop by restoring
    the compile half — 'the test source is buildable' — which is a guarantee the withdrawn lane used to
    carry implicitly.",

    "**WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED.** (1) Nothing is claimed about whether the tests would pass; B is a
    compile-only statement, which is precisely why the trigger is a narrowing. (2) Nothing is claimed
    about the whole workspace's test targets: the measured instance is `v1-compiler`'s lib test target,
    and a census over every declared test target is a larger claim this row does not make — a census
    with an entry point is the honest follow-up, and none exists today, which is the same deficit the
    hand-Rust ledger row records for scale (`gunbc.cli_run_hand_rust_area_ledger`, 'THE LEDGER HAS NO
    EXECUTABLE MEASUREMENT ROUTE'). (3) The specimen head's own required runs were *superseded* by a later
    push and this row does not claim they were green with the defect present; what the row claims is
    modal and stronger than any run — no required check CAN refuse the state, which is a property of the
    command set, re-derivable by the grep above, not an accident of one head.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.rung_drop.v1_required_lanes_withdrawn", decl_name: "v1_required_lanes_withdrawn"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.witness.compiler_gate_workflow", decl_name: "compiler_gate_build_step_name"),
  ],
}
